   "source": [
    "# 2. Histograms before weights\n",
    "\n",
    "A histogram answers a simple question:\n",
    "\n",
    "> How many entries fall inside each interval of a variable?\n",
    "\n",
    "For bin $b$, an **unweighted** histogram contains\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "N_b = \\sum_{i\\in b} 1.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This is a count of rows/events.\n",
    "\n",
    "That is directly meaningful for Data because one recorded event corresponds to one observed event.\n",
    "\n",
    "For MC, however, the number of generated rows is chosen largely for computing/statistical reasons.\n",
    "It is not generally equal to the number of events predicted in the experiment."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "720f0592",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Exercise 2.1 — Make your first histogram\n",
    "\n",
    "Generate two toy samples and fill in the histogram call for sample B.\n",
    "\n",
    "Questions to think about:\n",
    "\n",
    "- What does the height of each bin mean?\n",
    "- Does the area of an unweighted MC histogram represent a physical event yield?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "4de00bfd",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "toy_a = RNG.normal(loc=50.0, scale=10.0, size=10_000)\n",
    "toy_b = RNG.normal(loc=65.0, scale=12.0, size=10_000)\n",
    "\n",
    "bins = np.linspace(0, 120, 41)\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7, 5))\n",
    "ax.hist(toy_a, bins=bins, histtype=\"step\", linewidth=2, label=\"Toy A\")\n",
    "ax.hist(toy_b, bins=bins, histtype=\"step\", linewidth=2, label=\"Toy B\")\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel(\"toy observable\")\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel(\"entries\")\n",
    "ax.legend()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0604121b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Question 2.1\n",
    "\n",
    "Both toy samples contain exactly 10,000 rows.\n",
    "\n",
    "If these were two different MC physics processes, why would equal histogram integrals **not**\n",
    "imply that the two processes predict equal numbers of events in the detector?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "47e75a20",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Reference answer.** Equal raw MC entry counts only mean that the two samples were generated with\n",
    "the same number of simulated rows. The generation size is a computing/statistics choice. Physical\n",
    "production rates depend on cross sections, luminosity, efficiencies, and other corrections, so the two\n",
    "samples can represent very different expected yields."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "638aaf4a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 3. Why MC needs event weights\n",
    "\n",
    "For a very simplified MC sample with cross section $\\sigma$, generated event count\n",
    "$N_\\mathrm{gen}$, and target integrated luminosity $\\mathcal L$, a baseline event weight is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "w = \\frac{\\sigma\\,\\mathcal L}{N_\\mathrm{gen}}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Then\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\sum_i w_i = \\sigma\\,\\mathcal L,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "so the sum of weights has the interpretation of a predicted event yield.\n",
    "\n",
    "Real HEP event weights are more complicated. They can include, for example:\n",
    "\n",
    "- luminosity/cross-section normalization;\n",
    "- pileup corrections;\n",
    "- trigger efficiencies;\n",
    "- lepton efficiencies;\n",
    "- b-tagging scale factors;\n",
    "- generator/theory weights;\n",
    "- process-specific corrections.\n",
    "\n",
    "The important principle is unchanged:\n",
    "\n",
    "> **MC histogram bins contain sums of event weights, not merely numbers of MC rows.**"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d25adf0b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Exercise 3.1 — Weighted versus unweighted processes\n",
    "\n",
    "Two MC processes are generated with the same number of rows but have different cross sections.\n",
    "\n",
    "Compute their per-event weights."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "2db92d52",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "N_A = 20_000\n",
    "N_B = 20_000\n",
    "\n",
    "SIGMA_A = 10.0\n",
    "SIGMA_B = 2.0\n",
    "LUMI = 100.0\n",
    "\n",
    "x_a = RNG.normal(50, 10, N_A)\n",
    "x_b = RNG.normal(70, 15, N_B)\n",
    "\n",
    "weight_a = SIGMA_A * LUMI / N_A\n",
    "weight_b = SIGMA_B * LUMI / N_B\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Expected weighted yield A:\", SIGMA_A * LUMI)\n",
    "print(\"Expected weighted yield B:\", SIGMA_B * LUMI)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "dec4f528",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Once you have defined `weight_a` and `weight_b`, run the next cell."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "7623242b",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "if weight_a is None or weight_b is None:\n",
    "    raise RuntimeError(\"Complete Exercise 3.1 first.\")\n",
    "\n",
    "weights_a = np.full(N_A, weight_a)\n",
    "weights_b = np.full(N_B, weight_b)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"sum(weights A) = {weights_a.sum():.1f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"sum(weights B) = {weights_b.sum():.1f}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "bins = np.linspace(0, 130, 41)\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, (ax0, ax1) = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(12, 4.5))\n",
    "\n",
    "ax0.hist(x_a, bins=bins, histtype=\"step\", linewidth=2, label=\"A\")\n",
    "ax0.hist(x_b, bins=bins, histtype=\"step\", linewidth=2, label=\"B\")\n",
    "ax0.set_title(\"Unweighted MC rows\")\n",
    "ax0.set_ylabel(\"entries\")\n",
    "ax0.legend()\n",
    "\n",
    "ax1.hist(x_a, bins=bins, weights=weights_a, histtype=\"step\", linewidth=2, label=\"A\")\n",
    "ax1.hist(x_b, bins=bins, weights=weights_b, histtype=\"step\", linewidth=2, label=\"B\")\n",
    "ax1.set_title(\"Weighted physical prediction\")\n",
    "ax1.set_ylabel(\"weighted events\")\n",
    "ax1.legend()\n",
    "\n",
    "for ax in (ax0, ax1):\n",
    "    ax.set_xlabel(\"toy observable\")\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "35a91bd9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Question 3.1\n",
    "\n",
    "What changed between the two panels?\n",
    "\n",
    "Which panel should be used if you want to compare the predicted contributions of processes A and B\n",
    "to a real dataset collected at luminosity `LUMI`?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c9446f22",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Reference answer.** The weighted panel is the physical prediction. The unweighted panel gives both\n",
    "samples the same integral because they contain the same number of generated rows. After weighting,\n",
    "process A has five times the total expected yield of B because its cross section is five times larger."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b44350b0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 4. Weighted histogram uncertainties\n",
    "\n",
    "For an ordinary unweighted count in a bin,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "N = \\sum_i 1,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "the familiar statistical uncertainty is approximately\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\sigma_N = \\sqrt{N}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "For weighted MC,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "M = \\sum_i w_i,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "the corresponding statistical variance is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\sigma_M^2 = \\sum_i w_i^2,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "so\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\sigma_M = \\sqrt{\\sum_i w_i^2}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This is important because two bins can have the same weighted yield while having very different MC\n",
    "statistical precision."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "76f1231f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Exercise 4.1 — Same yield, different MC precision\n",
    "\n",
    "Consider these two bins:\n",
    "\n",
    "```text\n",
    "Bin A: 1000 MC events, each with weight 1\n",
    "Bin B:   10 MC events, each with weight 100\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "Both predict a yield of 1000.\n",
    "\n",
    "Calculate the statistical uncertainty for each."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "7f341be7",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "w_many = np.ones(1000)\n",
    "w_few = np.full(10, 100.0)\n",
    "\n",
    "yield_many = w_many.sum()\n",
    "yield_few = w_few.sum()\n",
    "\n",
    "unc_many = np.sqrt(np.sum(w_many**2))\n",
    "unc_few = np.sqrt(np.sum(w_few**2))\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Yield with many small weights:\", yield_many)\n",
    "print(\"Yield with few large weights:\", yield_few)\n",
    "print(\"Uncertainty with many small weights:\", unc_many)\n",
    "print(\"Uncertainty with few large weights:\", unc_few)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "605ecca7",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Question 4.1\n",
    "\n",
    "Why is the second prediction statistically much less precise even though its weighted yield is identical?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3370c80a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Reference answer.** The second bin has only ten statistically independent generated events. Its\n",
    "weighted yield is 1000, but its uncertainty is\n",
    "$\\sqrt{10\\times100^2}\\sim 316$, compared with $\\sqrt{1000}\\sim 31.6$ for the first bin.\n",
    "Large weights do not create additional Monte Carlo statistics."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "20d23034",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 5. Effective sample size\n",
    "\n",
    "A useful summary for a weighted sample is the Kish effective sample size\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "N_\\mathrm{eff} =\n",
    "\\frac{\\left(\\sum_i w_i\\right)^2}{\\sum_i w_i^2}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "If all weights are equal, $N_\\mathrm{eff}$ is close to the number of events.\n",
    "\n",
    "If a few events carry very large weights, $N_\\mathrm{eff}$ can be much smaller than the raw number\n",
    "of MC rows."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "f7b0eff0",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def effective_sample_size(weights):\n",
    "    weights = np.asarray(weights, dtype=float)\n",
    "    denom = np.sum(weights**2)\n",
    "    if denom == 0:\n",
    "        return np.nan\n",
    "    return np.sum(weights)**2 / denom\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"N_eff, many small weights:\", effective_sample_size(w_many))\n",
    "print(\"N_eff, few large weights:\", effective_sample_size(w_few))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8c005e3e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 6. Loading the real DY validation-region samples\n",
    "\n",
    "The project code relies on a single configuration (`c2st_config.py`) and an optimized loader\n",
    "(`dyvr_lib.py`). Check them out.\n",
    "\n",
    "We will load:\n",
