  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "50419dff",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Reference answer.** Good closure in one projection does not guarantee agreement in another.\n",
    "A correction can reproduce $p_T^{\\ell\\ell}$ while leaving jet multiplicity or correlations\n",
    "mismodelled. This is exactly the motivation for testing the joint feature distribution with a C2ST."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "96da9582",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 12. Shape agreement versus physical normalization\n",
    "\n",
    "There are two distinct questions:\n",
    "\n",
    "### Physical normalization\n",
    "\n",
    "> Does MC predict the correct absolute event yield **and** shape?\n",
    "\n",
    "Use the physical event weights as they are.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Shape normalization\n",
    "\n",
    "> If I ignore the total normalization, does MC reproduce the differential shape?\n",
    "\n",
    "For a shape comparison, normalize the total MC histogram to the Data integral in the displayed range.\n",
    "\n",
    "These questions must not be mixed.\n",
    "\n",
    "The Classifier Two Sample Test (C2ST) later uses global class balancing so that it primarily probes multivariate **shape**\n",
    "distinguishability."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b8d73608",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Exercise 12.1 — Build a shape-normalized comparison by hand\n",
    "\n",
    "For `2mu`, `mli_ll_pt`, and the **after** MC weights:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. histogram Data;\n",
    "2. histogram the total MC prediction;\n",
    "3. calculate\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "C = \\frac{\\sum_b N_b^\\mathrm{Data}}{\\sum_b N_b^\\mathrm{MC}};\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "4. multiply the total MC histogram by $C$;\n",
    "5. plot Data and normalized MC.\n",
    "\n",
    "Use the same 0–200 GeV range."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "5934b7be",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "channel = \"2mu\"\n",
    "mc, data = select_channel_tables(tables, channel)\n",
    "bins = np.linspace(0, 200, 41)\n",
    "centers = 0.5 * (bins[:-1] + bins[1:])\n",
    "\n",
    "data_values = np.concatenate([df[\"mli_ll_pt\"].to_numpy() for df in data])\n",
    "data_counts, _ = np.histogram(data_values, bins=bins)\n",
    "\n",
    "mc_counts = np.zeros(len(bins) - 1, dtype=float)\n",
    "\n",
    "for df, label in mc:\n",
    "    values = df[\"mli_ll_pt\"].to_numpy()\n",
    "    weights = df[\"weight\"].to_numpy()\n",
    "    counts, _ = np.histogram(values, bins=bins, weights=weights)\n",
    "    mc_counts += counts\n",
    "\n",
    "normalization = data_counts.sum() / mc_counts.sum()\n",
    "mc_shape = mc_counts * normalization\n",
    "\n",
    "if normalization is not None:\n",
    "    print(\"MC -> Data shape-normalization factor:\", normalization)\n",
    "\n",
    "    fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7, 5))\n",
    "    ax.step(centers, data_counts, where=\"mid\", color=\"k\", label=\"Data\")\n",
    "    ax.step(centers, mc_shape, where=\"mid\", color=\"tab:red\", label=\"MC after, normalized to Data\")\n",
    "    ax.set_xlabel(r\"$p_T^{\\ell\\ell}$ [GeV]\")\n",
    "    ax.set_ylabel(\"events\")\n",
    "    ax.legend()\n",
    "    plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "fc4e226d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Question 12.1\n",
    "\n",
    "Why can a shape-normalized plot look excellent even if the physical-normalization plot has a large\n",
    "Data/MC yield discrepancy?\n",
    "\n",
    "Which version should you use to answer each of these questions?\n",
    "\n",
    "1. “Does MC predict the observed number of events?”\n",
    "2. “Does MC reproduce the distribution shape?”"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "227922f9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Reference answer.** Shape normalization explicitly forces the displayed MC integral to equal the\n",
    "Data integral, so it removes any overall-yield disagreement. Use physical normalization to test absolute\n",
    "event yields; use shape normalization when the question is specifically whether the differential shapes\n",
    "agree after ignoring a global normalization factor."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8ca0f390",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 13. Negative MC weights\n",
    "\n",
    "Some Next-to-Leading-Order (NLO) MC generators produce signed event weights.\n",
    "\n",
    "A negative event weight is not a corrupt event. It is part of the cancellation structure of the\n",
    "higher-order prediction.\n",
    "\n",
    "For a physical histogram, the bin content is the **signed** sum\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "M_b = \\sum_{i\\in b} w_i.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The sum of absolute values,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\sum |w_i|,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "is useful diagnostically but is not the predicted physical yield."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "66446647",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Exercise 13.1 — Quantify negative weights\n",
    "\n",
    "For every MC table, calculate:\n",
    "\n",
    "- fraction of rows with `weight_uncorrected < 0`;\n",
    "- signed sum of `weight_uncorrected`;\n",
    "- sum of absolute `weight_uncorrected`;\n",
    "- fraction of total absolute weight carried by negative events.\n",
    "\n",
    "This connects directly to the later C2ST discussion, where ordinary binary cross entropy cannot simply\n",
    "use negative sample weights."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "2505bc22",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "for label, df in tables.items():\n",
    "    if label in cfg.DATA_PROCESSES:\n",
    "        continue\n",
    "\n",
    "    w = df[\"weight_uncorrected\"].to_numpy(dtype=float)\n",
    "    negative = w < 0\n",
    "\n",
    "    n_rows = len(w)\n",
    "    neg_row_fraction = negative.mean() if n_rows else np.nan\n",
    "\n",
    "    signed_sum = w.sum()\n",
    "    abs_sum = np.abs(w).sum()\n",
    "    negative_abs_sum = np.abs(w[negative]).sum()\n",
    "    negative_abs_fraction = negative_abs_sum / abs_sum if abs_sum > 0 else np.nan\n",
    "\n",
    "    print(\n",
    "        f\"{label:25s} \"\n",
    "        f\"negative rows={neg_row_fraction:8.3%}  \"\n",
    "        f\"sumw={signed_sum:12.2f}  \"\n",
    "        f\"sum|w|={abs_sum:12.2f}  \"\n",
    "        f\"negative share of |w|={negative_abs_fraction:8.3%}\"\n",
    "    )"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "74d029b7",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Question 13.1\n",
    "\n",
    "Why is\n",
    "\n",
    "```python\n",
    "df[\"weight_uncorrected\"].abs().sum()\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "not the physical MC yield?\n",
    "\n",
    "Why nevertheless is the absolute-weight fraction useful when deciding whether excluding negative events\n",
    "from NN training is a potentially important approximation?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "445d8de5",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Reference answer.** The signed sum is the physical NLO prediction because positive and negative\n",
    "contributions cancel as required by the calculation. The absolute sum removes those cancellations and is\n",
    "therefore not a physical yield. The absolute-weight fraction is nevertheless useful for quantifying how\n",
    "much statistical/phase-space importance is carried by negative events when NN training excludes them."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "39946223",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 14. An open-ended Data/MC exploration\n",
    "\n",
    "You now have the basic analysis pattern.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Exercise 14.1 — Choose one additional variable\n",
