    "reasonable change of binning."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "49ed5df7",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Exercise 14.2 — Which process dominates?\n",
    "\n",
    "For one channel and one variable:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. inspect the weighted yield of each MC process separately;\n",
    "2. rank the processes by total weighted yield in your displayed range;\n",
    "3. identify which background dominates;\n",
    "4. ask whether the dominant process is also necessarily the source of the largest Data/MC mismatch.\n",
    "\n",
    "Explain why “largest background” and “largest mismodelling source” are not the same concept."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8d19ca83",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Reference analysis.** Rank processes using their *weighted* yield in the displayed range. The\n",
    "largest background is the process contributing the most predicted events, but it is not automatically\n",
    "the most mismodelled process. A smaller component can have a much larger fractional modelling error and\n",
    "produce a visible localized discrepancy."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "20da88f9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 15. What comes next: the Classifier Two Sample Test (C2ST)\n",
    "\n",
    "One-dimensional histograms are essential, but they test one projection at a time.\n",
    "\n",
    "It is possible that\n",
    "\n",
    "```text\n",
    "Data and MC agree in x\n",
    "Data and MC agree in y\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "while disagreeing in the joint distribution\n",
    "\n",
    "```text\n",
    "(x, y).\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "A classifier two-sample test asks a broader question:\n",
    "\n",
    "> Given many observables simultaneously, can a classifier determine whether an event came from Data or\n",
    "> MC?\n",
    "\n",
    "With balanced Data/MC class priors:\n",
    "\n",
    "- AUC near 0.5 means little classifier-visible separation;\n",
    "- AUC above 0.5 means residual multivariate distinguishability.\n",
    "\n",
    "The C2ST does **not replace** the plots you made here.\n",
    "\n",
    "Instead:\n",
    "\n",
    "```text\n",
    "C2ST detects a discrepancy\n",
    "        ↓\n",
    "ordinary Data/MC plots help localize and interpret it\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "We are going to explore these concepts in more detail in the `toy_c2st_demo` example."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e3d75c58",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Final questions\n",
    "\n",
    "Before moving on, be able to explain in your own words:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. What is the difference between an MC row count and an MC predicted yield?\n",
    "2. Why is a weighted histogram uncertainty based on $\\sum w_i^2$?\n",
    "3. Why can two samples with equal `len(df)` have completely different physical yields?\n",
    "4. What is the difference between `weight_uncorrected` and `weight` in this project?\n",
    "5. What is the difference between physical normalization and shape normalization?\n",
    "6. Why are negative MC weights legitimate in a physical prediction?\n",
    "7. Why can one-dimensional closure plots miss a discrepancy that a multivariate C2ST sees?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8a25886e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Reference solution for the open-ended exploration\n",
    "\n",
    "The exact visual result depends on the production currently configured in `c2st_config.py`.\n",
    "The code below is the **expected analysis pattern**, not an expected fixed numerical answer."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "30b00511",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Example open-ended solution: MET after correction.\n",
    "#\n",
    "# If your configured selection already imposes MET < 200 GeV, this range matches that phase space.\n",
    "bins_met = np.linspace(0, 200, 41)\n",
    "\n",
    "for channel in (\"2mu\", \"2e\"):\n",
    "    mc, data = select_channel_tables(tables, channel)\n",
    "\n",
    "    fig, (ax, ratio) = dyvr_lib.plot_data_mc_ratio(\n",
    "        mc,\n",
    "        data,\n",
    "        \"mli_met_pt\",\n",
    "        bins_met,\n",
